<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dashnaktsutyun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Armenian Revolutionary Federation, or Dashnaktsutyun, is an Armenian nationalist and socialist party founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> in 1890. In 1917 and 1918 its members took part in the Transcaucasian governments and its armed units fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; it then led the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> until 1920. Its members are known as Dashnaks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation.jpg" alt="Armenian Revolutionary Federation" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeromi Mikhael · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armenian_Revolutionary_Federation_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the late 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> was the hub of small groups advocating reform in the Armenian-populated areas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. In 1890 Christapor Mikaelian, Simon Zavarian and Stepan Zorian united them in a new party, first called the Federation of Armenian Revolutionaries. Its declared goal was the political and economic freedom of Turkish Armenia by armed rebellion. Its first programme, written by Rosdom, mixed nationalist, socialist and Marxist elements, and it denounced Ottoman rule and the conditions in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> lived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Party members formed partisan groups, the fedayi, to defend Armenian civilians against the massacres and banditry common in some areas. In 1894 the party supplied arms to the people of Sasun against the Hamidian purges, and in 1896 it helped arm the defenders of Van. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> it kept to non-struggle until 12 June 1903, when the tsarist authorities decreed that Armenian Church property should come under imperial control. The party sent militiamen to guard churches, and in 1904 it turned to acts of terrorism against the imperial bureaucracy. In 1907 its Fourth General Congress decided to take part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Iranian Constitutional Revolution</a>.</p><p>In 1915 Dashnak leaders were deported and killed with other Armenian intellectuals, and the party led the defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> of Van during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> collapsed in 1917, Armenian, Georgian and Azerbaijani leaders created a Transcaucasian Federation, which lasted only three months. The party was then instrumental in forming the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 1918, which fell to the Soviet communists in 1920.</p><p>After the communists exiled its leaders, the party rebuilt in the diaspora, with a strong base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and to a lesser extent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and later in more than twenty countries. It worked to preserve Armenian cultural identity. It could not operate in the Armenian Soviet Socialist Republic and was banned there until 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The party joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-international">Second International</a> in 1907 and has been a full member of the Socialist International since 2003. In 1919 it adopted an independent and united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> as its programme, with borders based partly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> of 1920. It has long been the most influential party of the Armenian diaspora and campaigns for the recognition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>. Within the diaspora, its rivalry with other Armenian parties, notably the Hunchakians, sometimes turned violent, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in 1926 and 1958.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armenian Revolutionary Federation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In May 1918 the First Republic of Armenia emerged, governed by the <b>Armenian Revolutionary Federation</b> and burdened by refugees from the 1915 genocide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>None of the proposals succeeded; the <b>Armenian Revolutionary Federation</b> (Dashnaktsutyun), part of the governing coalition, resisted them.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mer-hayrenik">Mer Hayrenik (Our Fatherland)</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1918 the Baku Soviet, dominated by Bolsheviks and supported by <b>Armenian Dashnak</b> forces, defeated Azerbaijani Musavat units in fighting known as the March Days, in which thousands of Muslims were killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/baku-commune">Baku Commune</a></li><li><blockquote>Socialist Revolutionaries, Mensheviks and <b>Armenian Dashnaks</b> formed a new government, named after the Centrocaspian committee of sailors of the Caspian Fleet.</blockquote><a href="/state/centrocaspian-dictatorship">Centrocaspian Dictatorship</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was governed by the <b>Armenian Revolutionary Federation</b>, with prime ministers Hovhannes Kajaznuni and Alexander Khatisian and a parliament elected by universal suffrage, including women.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1918 Red Guards and <b>Armenian Dashnak</b> units from Tashkent stormed Kokand, killing thousands and burning much of the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kokand-autonomy">Kokand Autonomy</a></li></ul></section>
